From 48df0e953aaa4e34c6faa29a4ba045325895a3cf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: atomozero <bernardi.andrea@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 20:35:05 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 30/42] bluetooth_server: keep the bonds in the bond store

The server keeps one bond store and one device cache per controller
address, for as long as it runs; a controller takes them once it knows
its address, which the setup now reads (Read BD_ADDR), and takes the
answer to whoever asked first. A controller that comes back on the bus
takes the same ones again: the old one no longer writes the file over
the new one when it goes. They live in the system settings,
"bluetooth/<address>/".

Link key requests are answered from the store, new keys go to it with
what the pairing told (both sides' authentication requirements of a
simple pairing), and refused keys are forgotten by its rules. A key
from a pairing is kept also when the device was not in the list yet.
The paired devices are the bonded ones; names, classes and services go
to the cache. Removing a device removes its bond, also when it is not
in the list.

The first time an adapter has no bonds, those of Bluetooth_paired_devices
are taken in. The file stays as it is, as the Bluetooth audio add-on
still reads it, but the server no longer writes it.

Bond changes are sent to the watchers of BT_NOTICE_BONDS_CHANGED, from
the looper. The fingerprints of the keys (made, given to the controller)
are traced only with "trace keys" in the Bluetooth settings, replacing
the temporary trace.

Tested on the AC100: 2 bonds and 3 devices taken in, the keys unchanged
(same fingerprints), "keys" readable by its owner only, "bonds" without
keys, and the device that had no key no longer listed as paired.

Signed-off-by: atomozero <bernardi.andrea@gmail.com>
